  <p>
    The shortest path to a kid who thinks is a question you actually wait to
    hear the answer to. No place is that easier than bedtime, because the child
    is already quiet, already lying still, and you have nowhere to rush off to.
    If you use those five minutes to ask one or two real questions instead of
    filling the silence, you get something most schoolwork never touches: a
    child who reasons out loud. Here are the ten I reach for, and the four
    rules that make them work.
  </p>

  <h2>How to ask any of these (four rules)</h2>
  <p>
    The questions only work if you follow the rules, because the rules are what
    teach the thinking. Skip them and the questions collapse back into a quiz.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Ask one, then stop talking.</strong> A question is a gift of silence. If you answer it yourself or rush to the next one, the child learns there is no real question.</li>
    <li><strong>Never grade the answer.</strong> There is no wrong answer at bedtime, only a reason behind it. Find the reason with "what makes you say that?"</li>
    <li><strong>Let the silence sit.</strong> A child who pauses is thinking, not stuck. Give it a few long breaths before you gently repeat the question.</li>
    <li><strong>Follow the child, not the plan.</strong> If their answer opens a door, walk through it. The list is a starting point, not a checklist.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    Next question you ask, drop the yes or no version and ask the wide one
    instead. The whole method is in that one swap. The table shows what the
    difference sounds like at bedtime.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
  <table>
