  <h2>The ten questions</h2>
  <p>
    None of these has a single right answer, which is exactly why they work.
    You are not checking comprehension. You are giving a child practice at
    holding an idea, turning it over, and saying what they actually think.
  </p>

  <h3>1. What was the worst part of your day?</h3>
  <p>
    Start with the negative and you usually get a longer answer than the
    positive, because trouble is more interesting to remember. When they give
    it to you, do not fix it. Just ask: "If you got to do that part over, what
    would you do differently?" That is the question behind the question.
  </p>

  <h3>2. What is one thing you were wrong about today?</h3>
  <p>
    It is the rarest thing a child is ever asked, and the most useful. Getting
    comfortable saying "I was wrong" in year five is a gift that pays for
    decades. If they cannot think of one, offer your own mistake from the day
    first. Model it, then let them find theirs.
  </p>

  <h3>3. If you could change one rule at home, what would you change and why?</h3>
  <p>
    This is a child practicing how to argue with authority about a rule they
    actually care about. Do not agree or disagree with the answer. Ask them to
    defend it: "How would that rule work? What would happen to the part it is
    supposed to protect?" You are teaching that a good argument needs a reason,
    not just a want.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Was anything today unfair? What would have been fair?</h3>
  <p>
    Every child has a live sense of unfairness, and it is a philosophical
    superpower. What you want is the second half: not just "that was unfair"
    but "fair would have looked like this." Naming the fair version is where a
    child learns to build a case instead of just to complain.
  </p>

  <h3>5. What is something you noticed that most people walk right past?</h3>
  <p>
    This is pure observation training, the roots of both science and art. A
    child who practices noticing finds that the world is not boring, it is
    just underlooked. The answer can be a crack in the sidewalk or the way the
    dog turns around three times before lying down. The noticing is the win.
  </p>

  <h3>6. If you had to teach a robot one thing you learned today, what would you teach it?</h3>
  <p>
    Teaching is the fastest way to find out what someone actually understood.
    A robot asks no questions and knows nothing, so the child has to explain.
    If they cannot explain it to a robot, they did not fully learn it. That is
    a useful thing for them to discover gently.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Is a person brave because they are not scared, or because they are scared and do it anyway?</h3>
  <p>
    This one is a real philosophical question and a five-year-old can hold it.
    Do not push an answer. Children usually land on "scared and does it
    anyway," because that matches what they have felt. The reason they are
    right is courage does not exist without fear, and now they own that idea
    instead of just hearing it.
  </p>

  <h3>8. What makes something yours? What is the difference between yours and mine?</h3>
  <p>
    Ownership is one of the oldest questions there is. A child thinks about
    sharing and fighting over toys all day, so they have real material. You are
    not looking for the correct theory of property. You are looking for them
    to realize there is a question here at all, and that "it is mine because I
    want it" is not actually the whole answer.
  </p>

  <h3>9. If someone who had never met our family asked you what we are like, what would you tell them?</h3>
  <p>
    This asks a child to step outside their own life and describe it, which is
    the beginning of self-awareness. It also tends to be a warm one, a good
    one to end on. Whatever they say, you learn what they think a family is
    for, and that is worth a lot more than a status update on the day.
  </p>

  <h3>10. What is the question you wish someone would ask you?</h3>
  <p>
    The subtle one, and the best closer. You are telling the child that the
    questions are not a test aimed at them, but a door they can walk through
    themselves. Give them the first few nights to find one. When they do, they
    have just told you what is actually on their mind, without ever having to
    be dragged into saying it.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do with a strange or funny answer</h2>
  <p>
    Answer it at face value and follow it. A child saying "a rock is mine
    because I touched it" is not wrong, they are working with a theory. The
    worst thing you can do is laugh or correct it, because that teaches them
    thinking out loud is risky. Say "touching makes it yours, does it? What
    about something every person has touched?" and let the theory do the work.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this matters more than the answers</h2>
  <p>
    You will not turn a six-year-old into a philosopher in five minutes a night.
    What you are actually building is the habit of thinking out loud in front
    of you without fear. That habit, kept up through the teen years, is the
    difference between a teenager who disappears into their room and one who
    still talks to you. The questions are small. The habit is not. If you want
    the bigger picture on introducing ideas this way, our guide on
    <a href="/guides/how-to-introduce-philosophy-to-your-child">how to introduce
    philosophy to your child</a> walks through the reading side of the same
    habit. And for a bedtime book that keeps the door open all on its own, our
    <a href="/books/the-adventures-of-the-curious-kid">Adventures of the Curious
    Kid</a> is built around a boy who thinks by asking, page after page, the way
    these questions ask a child to.
  </p>

